    {
      "id": "common-comparison-mask",
      "severity": "must",
      "path": "methods/common-comparison-mask.md",
      "sha256": "bbb98200f52b4a692ce097704b8c438179a795a44bec6b856c03cf31931af4ea",
      "reason": "The question compares model nitracline fields with WOA23 and computes correlation/RMSE, so the fields must be placed on one grid and evaluated on a common mask, while WOA23’s land-sea mask should not be imposed where WOA23 is not part of the comparison.",
      "status": "condensed",
      "notes": [
        "Build one shared valid mask with `build_comparison_mask` over the four nitracline fields (WOA23 is part of the comparison, so its default `ocean_only=True` applies) and apply it with `.where(mask)` before the pattern correlation and RMSE; report the mask's cell count beside the metrics.",
        "The global/basin differences from WOA23 are differences of means each taken on its own grid; state that such a difference is not the bias over the shared mask."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "basin-and-region-definitions",
      "severity": "should",
      "path": "methods/basin-and-region-definitions.md",
      "sha256": "d0d90c32fab9f6e0a39d4f769a4e902e6412b2ad1b09d9c7d19f6bb3aa426611",
      "reason": "The requested Pacific, Atlantic and Indian basin means depend on which basin partition is used, and the question explicitly specifies the WOA23 basin mask with marginal seas folded into parent oceans.",
      "status": "condensed",
      "notes": [
        "Basin statistics use the WOA23 basin mask (`select_basin`, `load_basin_mask`), not the OMIP 10-region mask; state that marginal seas are folded into their parent ocean (default `include_marginal_seas=True`) and that the Southern and Arctic Oceans are separate basins, excluded here.",
        "For a 2-D field use the surface labels (`depth_dependent=False`), and use the same basin mask for models and observation — basin means from different masks are not comparable."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "omip-native-grid-vs-regridded",
      "severity": "should",
      "path": "methods/omip-native-grid-vs-regridded.md",
      "sha256": "8ff046ffbadc5794fcd2f8fe93d3bb947c736dec650aa3a3cd6e2412b7da8e68",
      "reason": "The models are supplied on native grids with their own areacello, so gradients and integrals should be computed natively and regridding used only for maps and pattern comparisons.",
      "status": "condensed",
      "notes": [
        "Compute each model's global and basin mean nitracline on its own native grid with its own `areacello`; regrid to the WOA 1° grid only for the maps, the pattern correlation and the RMSE on the common mask, and state the method ('conservative' going to the coarser grid, 'bilinear' fallback).",
        "The nitracline is a vertical-extremum diagnostic quantised by the vertical grid: where the profile is smooth the maximum can jump between levels, so read the maps together with the gradient."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "cmip6-bgc-units-per-volume",
      "severity": "must",
      "path": "concepts/cmip6-bgc-units-per-volume.md",
      "sha256": "30d729792e1986dd91c822785512ceabe336ef10a47f0a7db602802e5425d70b",
      "reason": "Nitrate in CMIP6/OMIP is stored per unit volume, while WOA23 observational nitrate is per unit mass, so the shared concentration unit in the section figure and any nitrate comparison must account for this conversion.",
      "status": "condensed",
      "notes": [
        "Depth of a maximum gradient is scale-invariant, so locate the nitracline from the native `no3` field with no density conversion; do not convert to µmol kg-1 before locating it (an in-situ density rescales each level differently and shifts the depth a little).",
        "For Figure 2's single concentration unit, convert with in-situ density (`compute_potential_density_anomaly` with `ref_pressure=None`) and state it (`attrs['density_assumption']`); the constant default density errs by about a per cent in the deep ocean and polar water."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "omip-weighted-means-and-integrals",
      "severity": "should",
      "path": "methods/omip-weighted-means-and-integrals.md",
      "sha256": "89de52676ae54db8f5897bc77a08c78de3f776696db0020935a38b790c302cbe",
      "reason": "Global and basin mean nitracline depths should be area-weighted over ocean grid cells rather than simple cell averages.",
      "status": "condensed",
      "notes": [
        "Every mean is area-weighted over valid cells: Σ nitracline·`areacello` / Σ `areacello`, with the basin mask applied on the same grid; `compute_basic_stat` `op='area_weighted_mean'`. Unweighted `mean()` over (y,x), or over latitudes on a regular grid, is wrong.",
        "Weight statistics too: `compute_difference_metrics` weights by area by default; `analyze_correlation` needs `weighting='area'` for a pattern correlation."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "monthly-means-are-time-averages",
      "severity": "must",
      "path": "pitfalls/monthly-means-are-time-averages.md",
      "sha256": "df6b5c23460f88f1522e5c6a665e1efbb8322c3ae876c885530f3019aa284ee0",
      "reason": "Nitracline depth is a nonlinear diagnostic derived from the monthly nitrate fields, so computing it offline from monthly means must be interpreted as the nitracline of the climatological nitrate field, not the average of monthly nitracline depths.",
      "status": "condensed",
      "notes": [
        "The nitracline is nonlinear in the mean field, so state the order used: average first, then locate the gradient (the like-for-like choice when the reference is a climatology); `temporal_aggregate` with `freq='mean'` day-weights the 1971-2000 months, an equal-weight `mean(dim='time')` is not a time average.",
        "Sub-monthly variance cannot be recovered from monthly means."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "missing-values-and-land-cells",
      "severity": "should",
      "path": "pitfalls/missing-values-and-land-cells.md",
      "sha256": "f576244f13dde06fed1777eaa9721c68844f61758f66fac704b44f1551e90df2",
      "reason": "CMIP6 land and below-sea-floor cells are marked with 1e20, and failure to mask them would contaminate the nitrate profiles and gradient search used to find the nitracline.",
      "status": "condensed",
      "notes": [
        "1e20 decodes to NaN via xarray; keep every reduction NaN-aware with weights masked to the same cells, so land columns stay missing rather than contributing 0.",
        "Build each field's valid mask from the field itself (masks differ between variables) and treat any near-1e20 output value as an unmasked fill."
      ]
    },
